The game has a lot of strong points, but the occasional crashes can be frustrating. In my 26h playing this game, it crashed 5 times. Most often because I alt + tab.
The strong points:
+ Great combat, with a decent amount of combos to use.
+ A great variety of weapons;
+ Good art style;
+ Interesting story;
+ Fun puzzles.
Weak points:
-  Occasional crashes. If you atl + tab you risk a crash;
- Sometimes you can get stuck in terrain or objects;
- Occasional fps drops. More often when you open doors.
Overall the game is a great hack and slash.

As always with SE, the writing was god-awful in a very special, unique way, but gameplay was solid, the challenge
and the world felt very immersive.
Pros:
- bona fide open-world Final Fantasy for real, you guys;
- unconventional in a lot of ways (e.g. Lightning acts as three characters in one with three seperate ATB gauges and stuff);
- quite a lot of things to do and quite some variety;
- Lightning is extremely customizable and open-ended;
- battles are actually challenging almost from the very beginning;
- most monsters being puzzles is nice;
- the cute integrated dress-up game is continuously entertaining;
- as you ride trains, visit cafes and stay at hotels, the world starts to feel actually immersive.
Cons:
- Square-Enix writing. It's a thing in its own. I can't tell why in 99% of their games they keep doing this sh*t where characters stand in front of each other, robotically state their motivations (sometimes the same exact thing again and again five or six times in a row, often repeated in 3-4 following cutscenes almost freaking verbatim) and then get "developed" by suddenly changing motivations to the opposite out of the blue. And don't give me this "it's the Japanese style of storytelling" b*llsh*t, because it isn't "Japanese" storytelling, it's fanfic-grade storytelling.
- MMORPG-ey sh*t (dozens of bring N things quests etc.; but very few are mandatory, so its o.k.).
